# Privo stop page

`https://privo.fyi/p/stop` is the "Whoa, wait a second!" interstitial. It shows visitors where a link is going and sends them on after 20 seconds, or immediately if they click **Proceed**.

## Using it with Privo short links

Nothing to do. `privo.fyi/{shortcode}` redirects to the stop page automatically:

```
privo.fyi/abcd12
  -> privo.fyi/p/stop?url=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com&viaPrivo=true
  -> https://example.com
```

### Link previews and SEO

`privo.fyi/{shortcode}` now serves a small HTML page (HTTP 200) whose `<head>` mirrors the destination, so shared links and search engines see the destination's identity rather than "Privo":

- `<title>` and `description`
- Open Graph and Twitter card tags (`og:title`, `og:description`, `og:image`, `og:site_name`, `twitter:card`)
- `canonical` pointing at the destination

People are sent straight on to the stop page. Crawlers and link unfurlers (Bluesky, Slack, Discord, X, iMessage and so on) read the tags instead.

How the data is collected:

- It is fetched once, when the link is created (`POST /api/link`), and stored in the `links` table. The API response includes a `preview` object.
- Order of preference: Open Graph, then Twitter tags, then plain `<title>` / `<meta name="description">`.
- If the destination can't be read (timeout after 3.5s, not HTML, blocked), the page falls back to the destination's hostname and never blocks link creation.
- Links created before this change are filled in automatically the first time someone visits them.
- For these visits the stop page explains: "You are viewing this page because the link you clicked was shortened using **Privo**."

Preview images are copied, not hotlinked:

- At creation the Worker downloads the destination's `og:image` into R2 and serves it from `https://privo.fyi/i/{shortcode}`. That URL is what goes in `og:image` / `twitter:image`.
- Limits: 5 MB, 3 second timeout. Only PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP and AVIF are kept. The type is checked from the file's bytes, not the server's headers. SVG is refused.
- If the copy fails (too big, blocked, not an image, no R2 binding), the destination's original image URL is used instead, so you still get a preview on sites that allow hotlinking.
- Images are served with `nosniff` and a locked-down CSP, cached for a day.
- The image is a snapshot from creation time. There's no automatic refresh and no cleanup when a link is deleted.

## Using it from an external site

Any site can send its visitors through the stop page without creating a Privo short link. Link to `/p/stop` with `viaPrivo=false` and the three required parameters:

| Parameter | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `url` | yes | Destination. Must be `http://` or `https://`, URL-encoded. |
| `viaPrivo` | yes | Set to `false` for direct use. |
| `sourceSiteName` | yes | Name of your site, shown to the visitor ("Coffee & Code is sending you to…"). |
| `sourceSiteUrl` | yes | Your site's URL. Must be `http://` or `https://`. Its host is shown as "Sent from …". |

Example:

```
https://privo.fyi/p/stop?url=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com&viaPrivo=false&sourceSiteName=Coffee%20%26%20Code&sourceSiteUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoffeencode.cc
```

If `url`, `sourceSiteName` or `sourceSiteUrl` is missing or invalid, the page shows an error naming the problem and does not redirect. If `viaPrivo` is omitted, it is treated as `false`, so the same rules apply.

### HTML

```html
<a href="https://privo.fyi/p/stop?url=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com&viaPrivo=false&sourceSiteName=Coffee%20%26%20Code&sourceSiteUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoffeencode.cc"
   rel="noopener">
  Visit example.com
</a>
```

### JavaScript

Build the link with `URLSearchParams` so everything is encoded correctly:

```js
function privoStopUrl(destination) {
  const u = new URL("https://privo.fyi/p/stop");
  u.searchParams.set("url", destination);
  u.searchParams.set("viaPrivo", "false");
  u.searchParams.set("sourceSiteName", "Coffee & Code");
  u.searchParams.set("sourceSiteUrl", "https://coffeencode.cc");
  return u.toString();
}

link.href = privoStopUrl("https://example.com/some/page?a=1&b=2");
```

## Notes

- Always URL-encode `url` and `sourceSiteUrl`. Destinations containing `?`, `&` or `#` will otherwise break.
- The page is `noindex` and sends no referrer.
- The stop page doesn't verify where a link came from, so `viaPrivo=true` can be set by anyone. It only changes which parameters are required.
- Visitors can report a destination from the page via `/p/abuse.html?url=…`.

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `p/stop.html` | The page and its logic |
| `p/stop.css` | Styles (Privo theme tokens) |
| `worker.js` | Serves `privo.fyi/{shortcode}` with the destination's SEO tags, scrapes metadata at creation |
| `add-link-metadata.sql` | D1 migration adding the metadata columns (run before deploying) |
| `add-image-cache.sql` | D1 migration adding `image_cached` (run before deploying) |

## Deploying

Create the R2 bucket and bind it in `wrangler.toml` as `IMAGES`:

```bash
npx wrangler r2 bucket create privo-images
```

```toml
[[r2_buckets]]
binding = "IMAGES"
bucket_name = "privo-images"
```

Then migrate and deploy (migrations first):

```bash
npx wrangler d1 execute <DB_NAME> --remote --file=./add-link-metadata.sql
npx wrangler d1 execute <DB_NAME> --remote --file=./add-image-cache.sql
npx wrangler deploy
```

If you already ran `add-link-metadata.sql`, skip it. Without the `IMAGES` binding the Worker still runs and falls back to the destination's original image URLs.